            "content": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<h2>3. Explain the difference</h2><p>Now explain the mechanism rather than only recognizing an output.</p><p>Predict first. With a partner, alternate explaining and checking; alone, write your reasoning before choosing Check.</p>"
              },
              "subContentId": "12be997c-9607-4ec5-a71c-c771aadb4772",
              "metadata": {
                "title": "1. Define the result",
                "license": "U",
                "authors": [],
                "changes": [],
                "contentType": "Text"
              }
            },
            "useSeparator": "disabled"
          },
          {
            "content": {
              "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
              "params": {
                "media": {
                  "disableImageZooming": false
                },
                "answers": [
                  {
                    "text": "A lazy quantifier always matches exactly one character.",
                    "correct": false,
                    "tipsAndFeedback": {
                      "tip": "",
                      "chosenFeedback": "Lazy controls which repetition counts are tried first. It does not set the count to one. Here, .*? matches the three characters red.",
                      "notChosenFeedback": ""
                    }
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "The dot cannot match &gt;.",
                    "correct": false,
                    "tipsAndFeedback": {
                      "tip": "",
                      "chosenFeedback": "A dot can match &gt; in this example. The greedy pattern includes an earlier &gt; inside its match.",
                      "notChosenFeedback": ""
                    }
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "The lazy quantifier tries fewer repetitions first, so the match ends at the first &gt; that allows the entire pattern to succeed.",
                    "correct": true,
                    "tipsAndFeedback": {
                      "tip": "",
                      "chosenFeedback": "Starting at the first &lt;, lazy .*? expands only as needed. The &gt; after red completes the pattern, so the match is &lt;red&gt;.",
                      "notChosenFeedback": ""
                    }
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "The question mark makes the closing &gt; optional.",
                    "correct": false,
                    "tipsAndFeedback": {
                      "tip": "",
                      "chosenFeedback": "This ? follows *, making that quantifier lazy. The closing &gt; is still required.",
                      "notChosenFeedback": ""
                    }
                  }
                ],
                "overallFeedback": [
                  {
                    "from": 0,
                    "to": 100
                  }
                ],
